  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 433 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low stretching from southeast\nMontana through eastern Wyoming into eastern Colorado, while\nhigh stretches from Saskatchewan southeastward into the Central\nPlains and Midwest. Upper level analysis places broad high\nencompassing the southwestern CONUS with flat ridge over the \nPacific Northwest into the Rockies, while a low remains over \nnortheast Manitoba into northwest Ontario. Over our area, quiet \nweather continues in west-northwest flow.\n\nFor today, warmer air lifts into the area as aforementioned\nsurface low meanders easterly, which also will result in\nincreased moisture mainly over eastern parts of our area. In the\nmeantime, the southwest (particularly the far southwest) remains\nvery dry. With the low passing by, winds shift northerly and\nincrease over the west by late afternoon, bringing near critical\nfire weather conditions to much of our far west. With that said,\nfocus then shifts towards south central North Dakota into the\nJames River Valley. With the increase moisture, expect increased\ninstability with latest guidance suggesting CAPE values around\n2000-2500 J/Kg by late afternoon/early evening, with 0-6 Km\nshear pushing around 40-50kts. This sets the stage for severe\ndevelopment but the fly in the ointment may be forcing, as that\nwill be rather limited though latest guidance does indicate a\ncouple weak short waves possibly moving through. Therefore,\nexpect convection to be mainly isolated, but if it does occur\nthe severe threat will be there. For now will advertise the\npotential for ping pong ball size hail and 60 mph wind gusts.\n\nTemperatures will be cooler for Sunday with a temperature\ngradient over the area as we expect mid 70s over the far\nnorthwest into the Turtle Mountains region, while 80s will\nstretch along and southeast of the Missouri River. Modest\neasterly winds should escort a little more low level moisture\ninto the southwest and result in higher humidity values.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are expected into the\nevening and possibly overnight as a series of modest short waves\nand an inverted through move in. \n\nTemperatures look to remain around to a bit below average on\nMonday and Tuesday, then cooling below average for the later\npart of the work week, while a variety of disturbances moving\nthrough west-northwest flow keep a daily chance for\nprecipitation in the forecast.",
